# Build artifacts
/bin/
/dist/
# `make build` writes to bin/; a binary at the repo root is always a stray
# artifact from a manual `go build ./cmd/...` and must never be committed.
/lolstats-aggregate
/lolstats-ingest

# Verbose agent output. Kept out of the repository on purpose: it is evidence
# for a single task, not part of the project's history.
/.agent-artifacts/

# Scratch written by parallel lanes while they work. The `.zz-` prefix is the
# convention; every path using it is scratch and none of it belongs to the
# project. Excluded by pattern rather than by name because the names change per
# lane. This matters: a lane that keeps a nested `git worktree` under the repo
# (`.zz-laneC-wt`) would otherwise be staged by a `git add -A` as a gitlink to a
# commit that nothing references, which is a broken submodule entry rather than
# a recoverable mistake.
# Evidence written by a lane. The convention is the gitignored
# `.agent-artifacts/`; this catches a lane that writes a `files/` tree instead,
# which is what happened once and left an untracked evidence directory in the
# repository root.
/files/
/.zz-*

# Local data / secrets
.env
deploy/secret.yaml
*.pem
*.key

# Local planning notes, never shared/committed
/backlog.local.md

# The served corpus the compliance gate reads, captured from a running tier by
# `make capture-served-pages`. It is a build artifact of a live process, in
# /bin/ above, and is never committed.
/bin/served-pages/

# IDE / OS
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store
*.iml

# `make build` writes to `/bin/`. `build/` is different, and it is now empty:
# it held the Docker image *contexts* (`build/caddy`, `build/site`) until the
# static tier was deleted on 2026-09-17 (plan.md D-9). This rule stays because a
# binary dropped there would sit in a directory that any future image context
# added back would send to the builder and bake into an image; a hand-rolled
# `go build -o build/lolstats-web` did exactly that once.
/build/lolstats-*

# `web/` was the retired Astro tree, built in order to be measured while it was
# being dismantled: `npm install` ran inside it and the serve-time gates read its
# `dist/`. None of those outputs was ever committed. The tree itself was deleted
# outright on 2026-09-18 - not one tracked file under `web/` remains - and the
# four rules below stay anyway, for the reason given at the foot of this block.
#
# These four rules are RESTORED, not new. They lived at `.gitignore:36-40` under
# a `# Web` heading until commit `1d670fe` ("move the Astro tree's data out from
# under web/") deleted them for a directory it had not deleted yet — at that
# moment `web/` still held 82 tracked files and was the tree lane M3 was still
# working in, so `web/` is exactly where `npm install` and `astro build` still
# ran. Measured consequence, with the rules gone: **10,862 untracked files** in
# the working tree (`web/node_modules/`, `web/dist/`, `web/.astro/`), one
# `git add -A` away from being committed as project history — the same class of
# trap as the `.zz-*` and nested-worktree-gitlink incidents above. Restored in
# `5524545` and `web/.vercel/` here, so that `git status` was a usable signal
# again while the tree was being dismantled.
#
# Keep them even after `web/` is gone: a rule for a deleted directory is a
# harmless no-op, whereas its absence is a live trap the moment anything
# recreates the path. The lesson is in the sequencing: an ignore rule may only be
# retired *with* the tree it protects, never before it.
#
# Note the root `/dist/` rule earlier in this file does NOT cover `web/dist`: it
# is anchored to the repository root.
/web/node_modules/
/web/dist/
/web/.astro/
/web/.vercel/

# Go
*.test
*.out
coverage.txt

# Python (used only for ad-hoc data inspection, never a dependency)
__pycache__/
